<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">WeVisOne-VL: Unified Visual Perception
<br class="ltx_break">from Objects to Pixels</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Tianming Liang<sup id="id1" class="ltx_sup">*</sup>, Junyi Pan<sup id="id2" class="ltx_sup">*</sup>, Yuxi Li<sup id="id3" class="ltx_sup">*</sup>, Canmiao Fu, Zhipeng Huang,
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:3.0pt;">
Chong Sun<sup id="id4" class="ltx_sup"><math id="m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dagger" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>†</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dagger</annotation></semantics></math></sup>, Chen Li<sup id="id5" class="ltx_sup"><math id="m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddagger" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>‡</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddagger</annotation></semantics></math></sup>, Jing Lyu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>WeChat Vision, Tencent Inc.
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Existing vision-language models (VLMs) remain unreliable for fine-grained visual perception.
In this report, we present <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">WeVisOne-VL</span> (WeChat Vision All-in-One), a powerful VLM that unifies object-level and pixel-level perception through structured next-token prediction. It supports multiple visual tasks including object detection, referring localization, counting, OCR, segmentation, and matting, enabling localization at any user-specific granularity, from points and boxes to masks and alpha mattes.
Architecturally, we integrate a lightweight pixel decoder into the VLM via a special <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;OBJ&gt;</span> token to generate instance-level pixel outputs. We train the model with a three-stage curriculum SFT followed by mixed-task RL.
Across over 30 benchmarks, WeVisOne-VL demonstrates leading performance on both object-level and pixel-level tasks, while maintaining robust rejection for object-absent queries.
By releasing WeVisOne-VL to the open-source community, we hope to advance VLMs from broad visual understanding toward truly reliable perception systems for real-world deployment.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Perceiving and understanding the visual world has long been a central pursuit of computer vision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">94</a>]</cite>. An intelligent visual system must be able to recognize what is present in an image, identify which object a user is referring to, and determine where these objects exist. Such capabilities establish the foundation for a wide range of real-world applications, such as content creation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite>, industrial inspection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, autonomous driving, and embodied intelligence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Over the past decade, computer vision has made remarkable progress toward this goal. From conventional detection and segmentation models (e.g., YOLO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib129" title="" class="ltx_ref">86</a>]</cite> and DETR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>), to open-world perception models (e.g., Grounding-DINO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>]</cite> and SAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>), and more recently to large Vision-Language Models (VLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, this field is being driven toward more unified, general, and powerful visual systems. This evolution has substantially broadened the concepts that a model can recognize and the instructions that it can follow. However, when both performance and efficiency are considered, current models are still far from satisfactory for real-world deployment. They often hallucinate non-existent objects, misinterpret user intent when encountering unfamiliar object descriptions, or overlook non-salient instances, leading to incomplete recall.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this report, we introduce <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">WeVisOne-VL</span> (<span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">We</span>Chat <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Vis</span>ion All-in-<span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">One</span>), a frontier vision-language model that unifies object-level and pixel-level visual perception through a single natural-language interface. The model casts diverse visual tasks as structured next-token prediction and incorporates a lightweight pixel decoder for generating dense outputs.
Built on Qwen3-VL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, we instantiate our models at two scales: 2B and 4B. We train the models on approximately 100M images with fine-grain annotations using a multi-stage curriculum. The key designs of WeVisOne-VL are summarized as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data Engine.</span>
We develop a unified data engine that connects semantic discovery with instance-aligned supervision for object detection, referring localization, segmentation, and matting. To guide targeted acquisition and task-specific annotation, the engine organizes category concepts and compositional referring expressions in a shared knowledge graph. It then combines complementary localization experts to generate spatial candidates and consolidates their predictions through visual-language verification of query–instance correspondence. Building on these verified localizations, the engine generates binary masks to extend supervision to the pixel level while preserving target identity across tasks, with manual spot checks for additional quality control. For matting, it further leverages expert predictions and curated data to support a dedicated annotator that provides continuous alpha supervision. Finally, the engine uses reviewed model errors to guide subsequent acquisition, annotation correction, and hard-example sampling, enabling iterative refinement of the training data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model Design.</span>
WeVisOne-VL consists of a VLM backbone and a lightweight pixel decoder. Built on Qwen3-VL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, WeVisOne-VL casts a broad range of vision tasks as structured next-token prediction, generating object labels and spatial coordinates autoregressively. For compact and efficient output, we introduce a group of dedicated tokens (from <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;0&gt;</span> to <span id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;1000&gt;</span>) to encode normalized coordinates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>. For dense prediction tasks, the model generates a special <span id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;OBJ&gt;</span> token after each predicted bounding box, whose hidden state serves as a latent representation of the corresponding instance. The pixel decoder combines this instance representation with multi-level features from the shared ViT to produce predictions over the full image canvas. In this way, WeVisOne-VL supports both discrete and dense predictions without relying on a separate large segmentation model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">122</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training Pipeline.</span>
We train WeVisOne-VL in a curriculum pipeline consisting of three-stage Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) followed by mixed-task Reinforcement Learning (RL). Stage 1 establishes task alignment, teaching the model to follow task instructions and produce well-formed structured outputs. Stage 2 introduces high-resolution and dense perception data to improve the recall of small and crowded objects. Stage 3 further incorporates pixel-level tasks, extending the model from discrete localization to dense prediction. For RL, we first select samples on which the SFT model is most uncertain, then apply Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) over the mixture of tasks. This on-policy optimization effectively improves the localization accuracy, while suppressing repeated or hallucinated predictions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Across multiple visual benchmarks, WeVisOne-VL demonstrates highly competitive performance against both state-of-the-art general-purpose models and specialized vision systems. It exhibits consistently strong capabilities on both object-level and pixel-level tasks, including object detection, fine-grained referring, text recognition and language-guided pixel prediction. In addition, it also shows strong potential in challenging real-world scenarios, such as maintaining high recall in crowded scenes and correctly rejecting queries when the target is absent. By unifying these capabilities into a single model, we offer a powerful, efficient, and versatile vision-language foundation for the open-source community, accelerating the development of truly practical and reliable visual systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>WeVisOne-VL Data Engine</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Data construction for unified visual perception must support both category-level recognition and context-dependent target selection, while preserving instance associations across language and spatial supervision. To this end, we build the <span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">WeVisOne-VL Data Engine</span> (WDE). WDE draws on GRIT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">78</a>]</cite>, CC3M <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib127" title="" class="ltx_ref">90</a>]</cite>, LAION <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib128" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>, SA-1B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, and several task-specific datasets, and its annotation inventory comprises over 100 million images and approximately 3.6 billion bounding-box annotations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2 WeVisOne-VL Data Engine ‣ WeVisOne-VL: Unified Visual Perceptionfrom Objects to Pixels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows an overview of WDE. We first organize category concepts and referring expressions in a shared knowledge graph, which guides targeted image acquisition (Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Concept Discovery and Targeted Acquisition ‣ 2 WeVisOne-VL Data Engine ‣ WeVisOne-VL: Unified Visual Perceptionfrom Objects to Pixels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>). The acquired image–query pairs then go through task-specific localization and semantic verification, which establish the correspondence between each query and its target instances (Sections <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Detection Annotation ‣ 2 WeVisOne-VL Data Engine ‣ WeVisOne-VL: Unified Visual Perceptionfrom Objects to Pixels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> and <a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3 Referring Annotation ‣ 2 WeVisOne-VL Data Engine ‣ WeVisOne-VL: Unified Visual Perceptionfrom Objects to Pixels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>). We keep these correspondences when generating binary masks (Section <a href="#S2.SS4" title="2.4 Mask Annotation ‣ 2 WeVisOne-VL Data Engine ‣ WeVisOne-VL: Unified Visual Perceptionfrom Objects to Pixels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>), and further extend the instance annotations to continuous alpha supervision with a dedicated matting pipeline (Section <a href="#S2.SS6" title="2.6 Matting Data Engine ‣ 2 WeVisOne-VL Data Engine ‣ WeVisOne-VL: Unified Visual Perceptionfrom Objects to Pixels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>). Finally, model errors reviewed after each training round guide subsequent acquisition and annotation refinement (Section <a href="#S2.SS7" title="2.7 Training Feedback and Data Refinement ‣ 2 WeVisOne-VL Data Engine ‣ WeVisOne-VL: Unified Visual Perceptionfrom Objects to Pixels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>). Below, we describe each component in detail.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.31329v1/data_engine.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/286;" width="476" height="286" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Overview of the WeVisOne-VL Data Engine (WDE).</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Concept Discovery and Targeted Acquisition</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Semantic discovery produces two complementary annotations: category-level concepts and compositional referring expressions. For detection, we use an optimized Tokenize Anything model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib124" title="" class="ltx_ref">76</a>]</cite> to discover object categories. For referring tasks, we prompt Qwen3-VL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> to generate referring expressions that specify targets through attributes, states, actions, and relations. Category concepts support category-based localization, while referring expressions support selecting particular instances from their context. Both serve as annotation queries, and their correspondence to image regions is established in the subsequent localization stages.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We organize both category concepts and referring expressions in a shared knowledge graph. The graph is initialized from resources including Wikipedia<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            <a href="https://www.wikipedia.org/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://www.wikipedia.org/</a></span></span></span> and WordNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib125" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite>, and represents canonical category names, aliases, taxonomic relations, and whole–part relations. A candidate term is first linked to existing nodes through text retrieval and vision-language verification, and is considered for addition to the graph only when it is supported by stable visual evidence. During normalization, we merge equivalent category terms while keeping the descriptive constraints that determine the scope of referring expressions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This semantic organization also supports targeted acquisition. Concept names and aliases serve as textual retrieval queries, verified instances provide visual retrieval cues, and historical errors indicate where additional coverage is needed. We set sampling priorities according to concept representation, image domain, target scale, and challenging conditions such as occlusion and dense object arrangements, and confusable categories and recurring failures in interpreting referring expressions further inform acquisition. In this way, annotation effort is directed toward both semantic gaps and visual conditions that remain difficult for the current model. The acquired image–query pairs are then sent to the detection or referring annotation pipeline.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Detection Annotation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Conventional object detectors excel at detecting densely distributed objects but struggle to recognize long-tail categories and open-world concepts. By contrast, VLMs offer strong recognition capabilities but often exhibit low recall in dense object scenarios. Therefore, for each object category, we first utilize detectors to generate exhaustive proposals, and then use a VLM for post verification. Specifically, we obtain proposals in parallel from SAM3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> and an in-house open-vocabulary detector based on a DINO-style architecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">123</a>]</cite>, which we refine iteratively. For each image–query pair, the predictions of both models are merged into a shared candidate pool. This design reduces our reliance on any single proposal source and uses the variation across detectors to pursue broader instance coverage.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For detection, we obtain proposals in parallel from two models: SAM3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> and an in-house open-vocabulary detector based on a DINO-style architecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">123</a>]</cite>, which we refine iteratively. For each image–query pair, the predictions of both models are merged into a shared candidate pool. This design reduces our reliance on any single proposal source and uses the variation across detectors to pursue broader instance coverage.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We then use Qwen3-VL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> to verify whether each proposed region matches the category query given the image content. Based on its judgments, we select candidates, remove duplicates, and resolve conflicting predictions. For the retained instances, we keep the bounding boxes produced by the localization models. These boxes serve as detection supervision and also define the target instances used in the subsequent segmentation annotation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Referring Annotation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Referring annotation requires the predicted regions to satisfy the compositional meaning of an expression, including the conditions that distinguish the intended targets within a scene. We build this stage on the SAM3 Agent framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> and add Rex-Omni <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> as an additional localization expert. Within the same expression-conditioned workflow, SAM3 and Rex-Omni both propose candidate regions, providing complementary localization evidence for the same expression.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Qwen3-VL then consolidates these proposals by checking whether each one matches the expression in its image context. The check covers the attributes, states, actions, and relations stated in the expression, and distinguishes the requested targets from objects that are mentioned only as contextual references. Whether an expression refers to a whole object or one of its parts is likewise determined by the expression itself. Conflicting proposals are resolved through the same semantic check. The accepted results bind each expression to an individual target or a target set, so that the annotation preserves the selection constraints of the query.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>Mask Annotation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each localized instance, we use SAM2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">85</a>]</cite> to predict a binary mask of its visible region. Each mask inherits the query of the box it is generated from. Each instance thus has both a box and a mask under the same query. With category queries, the boxes supervise detection and the masks supervise category-conditioned segmentation. With referring expressions, the boxes supervise referring localization and the masks supervise referring segmentation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We manually spot-check a subset of these annotations, checking whether each mask and box are linked to the correct instance and whether the masks are accurate. Defects found in this check are corrected through the refinement process in Section <a href="#S2.SS7" title="2.7 Training Feedback and Data Refinement ‣ 2 WeVisOne-VL Data Engine ‣ WeVisOne-VL: Unified Visual Perceptionfrom Objects to Pixels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>. We also record the source of every annotation, so that affected labels can be traced and regenerated.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.5 </span>Hard-Example Construction</h3>

<div id="S2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Some distinctions may not be adequately represented by broad semantic coverage alone, and we construct hard examples to focus on them. Candidate queries are derived from confusable concepts in the knowledge graph, visually similar categories, controlled changes to descriptive conditions, and observed model failures. These sources provide plausible alternatives that can only be handled by precisely interpreting both the category and the contextual constraints of a query.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A query is negative only when no object in the image satisfies all of its conditions, and this cannot be decided from the query or the detector output alone. For example, changing “red car” to “blue car” does not mean the image contains no blue car, and a detector that returns nothing for a query may simply have missed the target. Therefore, we review each candidate negative against the whole image, checking the attributes, actions, and relations in the query. If a matching target turns out to exist, the query is sent back to positive annotation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Whether an example is hard is determined by the behavior of the current model. A reviewed negative becomes a hard negative when the model nevertheless predicts a target region for it. Such examples carry an empty target set, so they teach the model to reject the query without introducing any box or pixel targets. We retain the erroneous predictions as evidence of the underlying confusion. Hard positives, in turn, capture missed targets, incorrect referent selection, and difficult localization under crowding or occlusion. We keep their reliable annotations, so that these examples strengthen the model’s sensitivity to true targets while hard negatives teach it to reject. Sample difficulty is reassessed for each model version, and the sampling emphasis is adjusted toward persistent and newly emerging failure modes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.6 </span>Matting Data Engine</h3>

<div id="S2.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We build a dedicated matting pipeline within WDE to provide WeVisOne-VL with alpha supervision across the diverse objects and scenes in our instance corpus. On the one hand, high-quality matting annotations are expensive to acquire, which limits their scale and coverage. Compositing foregrounds onto new backgrounds can enlarge the training set, but mismatched lighting and unnatural boundary transitions limit how well models trained on these images generalize to real scenes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">115</a>]</cite>. On the other hand, our instance annotations already identify each target and its spatial extent, but their binary masks lack the continuous alpha values needed for detailed boundary supervision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">115</a>]</cite>. Extending them to matting therefore requires recovering fine details while preserving the target specified by each description, even when the reference mask has a coarse or inaccurate boundary. To this end, we combine expert-assisted annotation, dedicated annotator training, and corpus re-annotation, producing alpha labels that remain associated with the original instance descriptions and bounding boxes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Expert-assisted Annotation.</span>
For each instance with binary mask <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain two alpha candidates on the same crop: the soft foreground mask <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>B</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{B}</annotation></semantics></math> from BiRefNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib126" title="" class="ltx_ref">125</a>]</cite> and the alpha matte <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>M</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> from MatAnyone2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">115</a>]</cite>. We initialize MatAnyone2 with <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> after dilation and erosion, repeat the crop as a short frame sequence, and take the final alpha prediction after warmup. BiRefNet, in contrast, predicts foreground from the RGB crop without any instance prompt, so its output may correspond to a different object or cover only part of the intended target. We therefore use its binarized foreground <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="B=\mathbf{1}[\alpha^{B}\geq\tau_{\alpha}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>B</mi></msup><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\mathbf{1}[\alpha^{B}\geq\tau_{\alpha}]</annotation></semantics></math> only to check spatial consistency with <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, we set <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\alpha}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\alpha}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> and accept a BiRefNet candidate only if all of the following criteria hold: <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|B|\geq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|B|\geq 8</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{IoU}(B,S)\geq 0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>IoU</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{IoU}(B,S)\geq 0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, coverage <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|B\cap S|/|S|\geq 0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|B\cap S|/|S|\geq 0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, foreground precision <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|B\cap S|/|B|\geq 0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|B\cap S|/|B|\geq 0.8</annotation></semantics></math>, area ratio <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|B|/|S|\in[0.5,2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|B|/|S|\in[0.5,2]</annotation></semantics></math>, bounding-box IoU of at least <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.7</annotation></semantics></math>, and a centroid offset no greater than <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.15</annotation></semantics></math> times the box diagonal of <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. For an accepted candidate, we further retain only the connected components of <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{B}\geq 0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>B</mi></msup><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{B}\geq 0.01</annotation></semantics></math> that have at least <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\%</annotation></semantics></math> of their area overlapping a slightly dilated <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. The alpha values within these components are preserved without clipping to <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, allowing fine structures to extend beyond the coarse boundary. If the BiRefNet candidate is rejected, or fewer than eight foreground pixels remain at threshold <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> after filtering, we fall back to <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>M</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m22" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\operatorname{IoU}(\mathbf{1}[\alpha^{M}\geq\tau_{\alpha}],S)\geq 0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>IoU</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>M</mi></msup><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{IoU}(\mathbf{1}[\alpha^{M}\geq\tau_{\alpha}],S)\geq 0.9</annotation></semantics></math>; otherwise, we retain <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. We record the selected source for each instance, together with any MatAnyone2 fallback to <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> caused by small crops or inference failures, so that model predictions can be distinguished from binary fallbacks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Matting Annotator Training.</span>
We train a dedicated matting annotator by sampling instances with initial labels and high-quality matting data in equal proportions, combining broad instance coverage with detailed alpha supervision. Inspired by SAM2Matting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">91</a>]</cite>, we adopt a frozen segmentation backbone with a trainable matting head. Specifically, we augment a frozen SAM2.1-Base+ model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">85</a>]</cite> with a new lightweight alpha refinement head. The head fuses multi-scale image features, prompt-mask guidance injected at each decoding stage, and local detail features from a shallow RGB stem, using top-down decoding to directly predict an alpha matte.
During training, prompt masks are obtained by binarizing the target alpha, taking the instance segmentation mask, randomly perturbing these masks, or segmenting the target with box-prompted SAM2. The objective combines multi-scale <math id="S2.SS6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and Laplacian alpha losses with foreground/background supervision and a prompt-consistency term applied only to unperturbed object-level prompts. Additional transition-region losses are applied to samples with continuous alpha labels, excluding small targets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corpus Re-annotation.</span>
Finally, we apply the trained annotator to all annotated instances in the training corpus, using the existing instance annotations to specify each target. Its predictions replace the initial labels and serve as the final alpha pseudo-labels for the matting training described in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Training Pipeline ‣ WeVisOne-VL: Unified Visual Perceptionfrom Objects to Pixels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Since each matte remains linked to its original instance description and bounding box, language grounding and matting are supervised on the same target.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.7 </span>Training Feedback and Data Refinement</h3>

<div id="S2.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After each training round, we analyze model errors to guide both data acquisition and annotation refinement. We organize the errors by concept, image domain, target scale, occlusion, task, and failure type, and apply clustering and deduplication to select representative cases for review. This concentrates annotation effort on recurring failures and coverage gaps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS7.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The review distinguishes errors in model prediction from errors in supervision. If the annotation is reliable, we keep the example unchanged; it may receive greater sampling emphasis or serve as a retrieval seed for related images. If the review identifies an annotation defect, we correct or regenerate the affected supervision before including it in a subsequent data version. In other words, model disagreement only triggers an investigation, and annotations are changed only after review.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS7.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Different failure modes lead to different updates. Missed targets guide additional acquisition and hard-positive sampling. Referent confusion motivates instance contrasts and more descriptive queries, while false positive responses contribute to hard-negative construction. Defects in boxes, masks, or cross-task instance associations are returned to the corresponding annotation stage for correction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS7.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS7.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Each data release records the annotation provenance, the model version that exposed each error, and the associated review and revision history. Unresolved cases are excluded from training. Original images, their identified near duplicates, and the derived task records stay within the same data partition across updates. Reviewed feedback data can enter subsequent training versions, whereas a fixed monitoring set is reserved for internal regression and excluded from both training and threshold calibration. Formal benchmarks are kept isolated from training and from feedback-driven data construction, so that evaluation remains independent as WDE evolves.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Model Design</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">WeVisOne-VL is built upon Qwen3-VL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> with two key extensions. (1) <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Task formulation</span>: it introduces dedicated tokens for normalized coordinates and task-specific tags, yielding a compact and efficient output format. (2) <span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model architecture</span>: it incorporates a lightweight decoder that transforms discrete token representations into dense predictions for segmentation and matting. Below, we provide the details of these two designs.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Task Formulation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We formulate all visual tasks as structured next-token prediction. Inspired by Rex-Omni <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> and LocateAnything <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">104</a>]</cite>, we introduce a group of dedicated tokens from <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;0&gt;</span> to <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;1000&gt;</span> to represent normalized coordinates, enabling higher token efficiency. As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.1 Task Formulation ‣ 3 Model Design ‣ WeVisOne-VL: Unified Visual Perceptionfrom Objects to Pixels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, WeVisOne-VL localizes queried targets by sequentially generating object references and spatial coordinates. For tasks requiring bounding boxes, we encode the spatial coordinates as <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[x_{0},y_{0},x_{1},y_{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[x_{0},y_{0},x_{1},y_{1}]</annotation></semantics></math>. For tasks requiring point outputs, we use <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[x_{0},y_{0}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[x_{0},y_{0}]</annotation></semantics></math> as the coordinates. If a queried target is absent from the image, its coordinate field is filled by <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">None</span>. For pixel-level tasks such as segmentation and matting, WeVisOne-VL additionally generates an <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;OBJ&gt;</span> token immediately after each box-coordinate quartet, inside the same <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;box&gt;</span> field, to provide the target representation for pixel decoding.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Representative input–output examples for the unified task formats used by WeVisOne-VL.</figcaption><img src="2610.31329v1/template.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/282;" width="476" height="282" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Model Architecture</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given an image and an instruction, WeVisOne-VL first encodes the image with the Qwen3-VL vision encoder and autoregressively generates the structured response described above. For pixel-level tasks, it invokes a lightweight decoder for each generated <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;OBJ&gt;</span> token. The decoder takes the token’s hidden state, its predicted box, and multi-level features from the Qwen3-VL vision encoder. The decoder draws image context from the shared visual features and spatial guidance from the predicted boxes, eliminating the need for a separate large segmentation model such as SAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.31329v1/model.png" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/236;" width="476" height="236" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Overview of the lightweight pixel decoder. Qwen3-VL provides multi-level ViT features, the hidden state of each generated <span id="S3.F4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;OBJ&gt;</span> token, and its predicted box. The decoder builds shared visual features, updates a box-guided target query, and converts the two representations into a full-resolution mask or alpha matte.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The decoder consists of three components: a visual feature adapter that organizes intermediate ViT features into a shared multi-level memory, a deformable query decoder that combines each <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;OBJ&gt;</span> representation with its box prior, and an instance-conditioned dense prediction stage that converts the updated query and visual features into a full-image prediction. Segmentation and matting share these components, while matting uses an additional lightweight refinement module for fine boundaries and fractional opacity. The entire decoder, including the refinement module, contains about 9.6M parameters.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1 </span>Visual Feature Adapter</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To preserve fine-grained visual information for dense prediction, we extract <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> pre-merging features <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{F_{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times H_{p}\times W_{p}}\}_{l=1}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{F_{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times H_{p}\times W_{p}}\}_{l=1}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math> from the intermediate ViT layers, serving as multi-level visual features. For each level <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>, the features are mapped to a <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional decoder space and combined with positional and level information:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{l}^{(0)}=W_{l}F_{l},\qquad E_{l}=P_{\mathrm{2D}}+e_{l}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{l}^{(0)}=W_{l}F_{l},\qquad E_{l}=P_{\mathrm{2D}}+e_{l},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times C}</annotation></semantics></math> is a learnable projection, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\mathrm{2D}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times H_{p}\times W_{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathrm{2D}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times H_{p}\times W_{p}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the 2D positional encoding, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is a learnable level embedding. The visual feature adapter is composed of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> blocks. Given the input <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{l}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{l}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th block computes as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{gathered}\bar{X}_{l}^{(t)}=\operatorname{LayerNorm}\!\left(X_{l}^{(t)}+\operatorname{PWConv}_{t}\!\left(\operatorname{GELU}\!\left(\operatorname{DWConv}_{t}(X_{l}^{(t)}+E_{l})\right)\right)\right),\\
X_{l}^{(t+1)}=\operatorname{LayerNorm}\!\left(\bar{X}_{l}^{(t)}+\operatorname{FFN}_{t}(\bar{X}_{l}^{(t)})\right).\end{gathered}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:5.094em;" width="5.094em"><mi>LayerNorm</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mpadded style="width:4.086em;" width="4.086em"><mi>PWConv</mi></mpadded><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:2.714em;" width="2.714em"><mi>GELU</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>DWConv</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:5.094em;" width="5.094em"><mi>LayerNorm</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>FFN</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{gathered}\bar{X}_{l}^{(t)}=\operatorname{LayerNorm}\!\left(X_{l}^{(t)}+\operatorname{PWConv}_{t}\!\left(\operatorname{GELU}\!\left(\operatorname{DWConv}_{t}(X_{l}^{(t)}+E_{l})\right)\right)\right),\\
X_{l}^{(t+1)}=\operatorname{LayerNorm}\!\left(\bar{X}_{l}^{(t)}+\operatorname{FFN}_{t}(\bar{X}_{l}^{(t)})\right).\end{gathered}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The final outputs <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{X_{l}^{(T)}\}_{l=1}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{X_{l}^{(T)}\}_{l=1}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math> across different levels are then flattened along the spatial dimensions and concatenated to derive the visual memory <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}\in\mathbb{R}^{LH_{p}W_{p}\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}\in\mathbb{R}^{LH_{p}W_{p}\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2 </span>Box-guided Query Decoder</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our query decoder follows the design of Deformable DETR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">129</a>]</cite>, but differs in the initialization of instance queries: instead of using static learned embeddings, our queries are instantiated directly from the VLM outputs. Consider a response containing <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> predicted instances, each represented by a bounding box followed by an <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;OBJ&gt;</span> token. For the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th instance, let <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the hidden state of its <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;OBJ&gt;</span> token and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}=[x_{i1},y_{i1},x_{i2},y_{i2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}=[x_{i1},y_{i1},x_{i2},y_{i2}]</annotation></semantics></math> its normalized box. We initialize the content query as <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}=W_{h}\,h_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}=W_{h}\,h_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and the positional query as <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}=\operatorname{MLP}(\phi(b_{i}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>MLP</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}=\operatorname{MLP}(\phi(b_{i}))</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> denotes Fourier encoding.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our decoder consists of six deformable blocks, each containing a self-attention module, a multi-scale deformable cross-attention module, and a feed-forward network. In the self-attention module, the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> instance queries interact with one another, where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}+p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}+p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> serves as both the query and the key, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> serves as the value. In cross-attention, each instance query aggregates visual features from the memory <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> through multi-scale deformable attention conditioned on its reference box <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}=[\frac{x_{i1}+x_{i2}}{2},\frac{y_{i1}+y_{i2}}{2},x_{i2}-x_{i1},y_{i2}-y_{i1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}=[\frac{x_{i1}+x_{i2}}{2},\frac{y_{i1}+y_{i2}}{2},x_{i2}-x_{i1},y_{i2}-y_{i1}]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.3 </span>Instance-Conditioned Dense Prediction</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the multi-level visual features from the visual feature adapter and the query embeddings from the box-guided query decoder, this stage fuses the visual features, predicts an initial mask for each instance, and refines it across multiple scales.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multi-level Pixel Fusion.</span> Deep features encode high-level semantic information, while shallow features preserve fine-grained spatial details. To combine visual information from different levels, we first fuse the adapted visual features <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{X_{l}^{(T)}\}_{l=1}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{X_{l}^{(T)}\}_{l=1}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math> from top to bottom:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{l}=\begin{cases}\operatorname{Conv}_{1\times 1}\!\left(X_{L}^{(T)}\right),&amp;l=L,\\[4.0pt]
\operatorname{GELU}\!\left(\operatorname{GroupNorm}\!\left(\operatorname{Conv}_{3\times 3}\!\left(G_{l+1}+\operatorname{Conv}_{1\times 1}\!\left(X_{l}^{(T)}\right)\right)\right)\right),&amp;l=L-1,\ldots,1.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Conv</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:2.884em;" width="2.884em"><mi>GELU</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:5.410em;" width="5.410em"><mi>GroupNorm</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Conv</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Conv</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{l}=\begin{cases}\operatorname{Conv}_{1\times 1}\!\left(X_{L}^{(T)}\right),&amp;l=L,\\[4.0pt]
\operatorname{GELU}\!\left(\operatorname{GroupNorm}\!\left(\operatorname{Conv}_{3\times 3}\!\left(G_{l+1}+\operatorname{Conv}_{1\times 1}\!\left(X_{l}^{(T)}\right)\right)\right)\right),&amp;l=L-1,\ldots,1.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">To recover color, texture, and edge details that may be lost in the fused visual feature, we additionally resize the input image <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1024\times 1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1024</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1024\times 1024</annotation></semantics></math> and feed it into a two-layer CNN to extract a shallow feature map. Then, we upsample <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to the same resolution <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="512\times 512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>512</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512\times 512</annotation></semantics></math> and combine the two features by element-wise addition:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E4X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\operatorname{Up}(G_{1})+\operatorname{CNN}(I)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Up</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>CNN</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\operatorname{Up}(G_{1})+\operatorname{CNN}(I).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dynamic Mask Head.</span> Let <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{q}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{q}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the query representation of the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th instance derived from the box-guided query decoder. We first apply an MLP to <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{q}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{q}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to generate an instance-aware dynamic kernel, which is then applied to <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> to produce pixel-wise logits:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[k_{i},\beta_{i}]=\operatorname{MLP}(\widetilde{q}_{i}),\qquad M_{i}=k_{i}^{\top}P+\beta_{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>MLP</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[k_{i},\beta_{i}]=\operatorname{MLP}(\widetilde{q}_{i}),\qquad M_{i}=k_{i}^{\top}P+\beta_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Coarse-to-fine Refinement.</span> A single mask prediction is often insufficient for thin structures and translucent boundaries. Starting from <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i}^{(0)}=M_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i}^{(0)}=M_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we progressively refine the mask through three upsampling stages (<math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=1,2,3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=1,2,3</annotation></semantics></math>). At stage <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, we upsample the previous stage feature <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{i}^{(j-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{i}^{(j-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> and concatenate it with the resized image <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>, pixel feature <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, and previous mask predictions <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}^{(j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}^{(j)}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{i}^{(j)}=\operatorname{Conv}_{1\times 1}\!\left(\operatorname{Up}(F_{i}^{(j-1)})\mathbin{\|}\operatorname{Interp}_{j}(I)\mathbin{\|}\operatorname{Interp}_{j}(P)\mathbin{\|}C_{i}^{(j)}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Conv</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Up</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Interp</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Interp</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∥</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{i}^{(j)}=\operatorname{Conv}_{1\times 1}\!\left(\operatorname{Up}(F_{i}^{(j-1)})\mathbin{\|}\operatorname{Interp}_{j}(I)\mathbin{\|}\operatorname{Interp}_{j}(P)\mathbin{\|}C_{i}^{(j)}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Up}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Up</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Up}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes transposed convolution, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Interp}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Interp</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Interp}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes bilinear interpolation to the resolution of stage <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|</annotation></semantics></math> denotes channel concatenation. We then modulate <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{i}^{(j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{i}^{(j)}</annotation></semantics></math> with the instance query <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{q}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{q}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> via channel-wise scale and shift, and pass it through residual convolutional blocks <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathrm{rc}}^{(j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>rc</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathrm{rc}}^{(j)}</annotation></semantics></math> to predict a logit residual:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\gamma_{i}^{(j)},\delta_{i}^{(j)}]=\operatorname{MLP}_{j}(\widetilde{q}_{i}),\qquad F_{i}^{(j)}=f_{\mathrm{rc}}^{(j)}\!\left(\big(1+\operatorname{tanh}(\gamma_{i}^{(j)})\big)\odot U_{i}^{(j)}+\delta_{i}^{(j)}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>MLP</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>rc</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>tanh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\gamma_{i}^{(j)},\delta_{i}^{(j)}]=\operatorname{MLP}_{j}(\widetilde{q}_{i}),\qquad F_{i}^{(j)}=f_{\mathrm{rc}}^{(j)}\!\left(\big(1+\operatorname{tanh}(\gamma_{i}^{(j)})\big)\odot U_{i}^{(j)}+\delta_{i}^{(j)}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i}^{(j)}=\operatorname{Interp}_{j}\big(M_{i}^{(j-1)}\big)+\operatorname{Conv}_{1\times 1}\big(F_{i}^{(j)}\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Interp</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Conv</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i}^{(j)}=\operatorname{Interp}_{j}\big(M_{i}^{(j-1)}\big)+\operatorname{Conv}_{1\times 1}\big(F_{i}^{(j)}\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Finally, the output prediction is obtained by <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}=\operatorname{Sigmoid}(M_{i}^{(3)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Sigmoid</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}=\operatorname{Sigmoid}(M_{i}^{(3)})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Training Pipeline</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We train WeVisOne-VL with a three-stage Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT)
curriculum followed by mixed-task Reinforcement Learning (RL). The curriculum
first establishes a shared output interface, then improves high-resolution and
dense perception, and finally connects autoregressive localization with
pixel-level prediction. After SFT, RL directly optimizes the quality of the
complete prediction rather than the likelihood of individual output tokens.
The data composition across the training pipeline is summarized in
Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4 Training Pipeline ‣ WeVisOne-VL: Unified Visual Perceptionfrom Objects to Pixels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Training data composition. The SFT and RL rings show task distributions. The segmentation warm-up and matting fine-tuning rings show data-source proportions: Public vs. WDE (WeVisOne-VL Data Engine). Center numbers indicate training sample counts.</figcaption><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.31329v1/training_data.svg" id="S4.T1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="width:44.570em; height:14.089em;aspect-ratio:592/187;" width="592" height="187"></object>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Curriculum Supervised Fine-Tuning</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 1: Task Alignment.</span>
This stage adapts the model to the new tokens and task-specific structured outputs. Starting from Qwen3-VL with an extended vocabulary, we freeze the vision encoder and visual-language projector and train only the language model on 90M examples covering all target tasks for 13K optimization steps. The model learns to follow different task instructions, produce the structured outputs, and return <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">None</span> when the target is absent. For pixel-task examples, we impose only next-token prediction supervision (NTP) without activating the pixel decoder, thereby establishing an initial correspondence between each predicted instance and its <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;OBJ&gt;</span> token. In this stage, we use 16–512 visual tokens per image and limit the context length to 4,096 tokens. Notably, early training can be dominated by extremely dense samples (e.g., containing hundreds of boxes in a single response), making the model highly prone to repetitive output collapse. To mitigate this issue, we primarily use samples with at most 50 boxes or points per category during this stage.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 2: Resolution and Density Scaling.</span>
Building on Stage 1, we unfreeze the vision encoder, projector, and language model. This stage serves two purposes. First, we raise the maximum input resolution to 1,960 visual tokens, preserving the visual details for recognizing small objects. Second, we increase the maximum box/point number within one response to 500, exposing the model to crowded scenes for dense detection. The maximum context length is extended to 8,192 tokens. We train on approximately 40M examples for 9K optimization steps. During this stage, pixel-level examples remain supervised solely by NTP.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 3: Alternating Token–Pixel Optimization.</span>
This stage aims to equip the VLM with pixel-level perception by establishing the <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;OBJ&gt;</span> token as the bridge between the VLM and the lightweight pixel decoder. To initialize the pixel decoder before alternating token–pixel training, we first jointly train the SFT-S2 VLM and the randomly initialized decoder on 250M segmentation samples for 100K steps with the following segmentation loss function:</p>
<table id="S4.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{seg}}=20\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{focal}}+\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{dice}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>seg</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>focal</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>dice</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{seg}}=20\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{focal}}+\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{dice}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where the focal term emphasizes hard pixels under foreground–background imbalance, while the Dice term directly optimizes mask overlap. After warm-up, we retain only the decoder weights and the learned <span id="S4.SS1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;OBJ&gt;</span> token embedding. We attach them to the original Stage 2 checkpoint and jointly train the VLM and decoder for 9,000 steps. To preserve localization ability, we alternate NTP and pixel-level tasks at a 1:1 ratio, which can be expressed as the following loss function:</p>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{joint}}=\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{NTP}}+10\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{seg}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>joint</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>NTP</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>seg</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{joint}}=\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{NTP}}+10\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{seg}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">After the alternating training, we specialize the pixel decoder for continuous alpha prediction while keeping the VLM frozen. We use 130M matting samples to further finetune the decoder for 100K steps. Following standard practice in image matting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>, we combine <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, gradient, and Laplacian losses to supervise alpha estimation, and additionally employ <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{hard}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>hard</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{hard}}</annotation></semantics></math> to emphasize difficult foreground and background pixels:</p>
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{mat}}=200\mathcal{L}_{l1}+5\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{grad}}+50\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{lap}}+\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{hard}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>mat</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>200</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>grad</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>lap</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>hard</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{mat}}=200\mathcal{L}_{l1}+5\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{grad}}+50\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{lap}}+\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{hard}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Mixed-Task Reinforcement Learning</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although SFT provides a strong initialization, its token-level likelihood
objective cannot directly optimize task rewards. We therefore perform
mixed-task reinforcement learning on an uncertainty-filtered corpus.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Uncertainty-Aware Sample Selection.</span>
GRPO requires relative advantages to provide learning signals. Before RL training, we therefore use the SFT model for data sampling. For each candidate prompt <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, we sample a group of responses and compute their corresponding task rewards. Let <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the mean and standard deviation of the group rewards, respectively. The uncertainty score for each sample is defined as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U(x)=4\mu_{x}(1-\mu_{x})\sigma_{x}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U(x)=4\mu_{x}(1-\mu_{x})\sigma_{x}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This score favors moderately difficult samples with high reward variance, on which the model succeeds in some rollouts but fails in others. Such samples have high uncertainty and yield stronger relative-advantage signals, thereby improving the efficiency of RL training. Using this score, we select approximately 140K samples for RL training. We supplement this mixture with targeted box-supervised examples and pointing data, yielding 178K examples in total. Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4 Training Pipeline ‣ WeVisOne-VL: Unified Visual Perceptionfrom Objects to Pixels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows the complete task distribution. Data selection and reward details are provided in Appendices <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Reinforcement Learning Data ‣ Appendix A WeVisOne-VL Training Corpus ‣ WeVisOne-VL: Unified Visual Perceptionfrom Objects to Pixels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a> and <a href="#A2.SS3" title="B.3 RL Rewards ‣ Appendix B Implementation Details ‣ WeVisOne-VL: Unified Visual Perceptionfrom Objects to Pixels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GRPO with Grounding Rewards.</span>
On the resulting training corpus, we perform GRPO using grounding rewards computed over complete structured responses. These rewards directly evaluate the grounding quality of the model predictions rather than individual tokens.
For each selected image–prompt pair <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, we sample <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=8</annotation></semantics></math> complete responses <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{o_{i}\}_{i=1}^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{o_{i}\}_{i=1}^{G}</annotation></semantics></math> from the current policy and parse each response into a set of predicted objects. Let <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{B}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{B}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the predicted and ground-truth box sets, respectively. We perform one-to-one Hungarian matching between predicted and ground-truth boxes within each semantic class, and denote the set of matched pairs as <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The grounding reward for response <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as:</p>
<table id="S4.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
