<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate WeVisOne-VL across five object-level tasks (object detection, referring object detection, object counting, object pointing, and OCR) and two pixel-level tasks (referring segmentation and image matting). At inference, we use greedy decoding with temperature 0 and limit each image to 1,960 visual tokens.
We primarily compare with the Qwen series as general-purpose VLM baselines and with two representative VLM-based grounding models, Rex-Omni <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> and LocateAnything <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">104</a>]</cite>. For Qwen3-VL, we evaluate the Instruct variants; for Qwen3.5, we enable thinking mode because it performs better in our evaluation.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Main Results</h3>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.1 </span>Object Detection</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benchmarks.</span>
We evaluate common object detection on COCO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite>, long-tailed object detection on LVIS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, and dense and tiny-object detection on Dense200 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> and VisDrone <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metrics.</span>
Average Precision (AP) relies on calibrated confidence scores and is therefore not directly applicable to generative VLMs. Following Rex-Omni <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> and LocateAnything <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">104</a>]</cite>, we report F1 scores. A predicted box is regarded as a true positive if its IoU with an unmatched ground-truth box of the same category exceeds a threshold. Precision and recall are computed per category and averaged, and F1 is their harmonic mean. We report F1 at IoU=0.5, at IoU=0.95, and averaged over IoU thresholds from 0.5 to 0.95 with a step of 0.05 (denoted as “Mean”).</p>
</div>
