<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span> As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.1.1 Object Detection ‣ 5.1 Main Results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ WeVisOne-VL: Unified Visual Perceptionfrom Objects to Pixels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, WeVisOne-VL consistently outperforms VLMs at comparable model scales in mean F1 across general, long-tailed, and dense detection benchmarks. The gains are especially pronounced on Dense200 and VisDrone, where accurate recognition must be maintained over many small and crowded instances. Its strong performance under the strict IoU<math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}={}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}={}</annotation></semantics></math>0.95 criterion further indicates that the improvement reflects both broad object coverage and precise localization.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.2 </span>Referring Object Detection</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benchmarks.</span>
We evaluate referring object detection on the public RefCOCOg <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>]</cite>, HumanRef <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>, Ref-Adv <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, PR-Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, DVGBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">127</a>]</cite>, and Ref-L4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> benchmarks. We also include RefCOCOg-Rej <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite>, in which each modified expression has no matching object in the image. Furthermore, we introduce WeRef-Bench, an in-house benchmark comprising 2,880 English and Chinese queries. It covers single-target referring, multi-target referring, and absent-target queries, providing more challenging and practical evaluations than the public positive, single-target benchmarks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metrics.</span>
For most benchmarks, we report F1 Mean as the primary metric. For RefCOCOg-Rej, we report the Rejection Rate, where a prediction is correct only when no box is produced. For WeRef-Bench and PR-Bench, we report both F1 Mean and Rejection Rate to evaluate target-present and absent-target queries, respectively.</p>
</div>
