<section id="S5.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.3 </span>Object Counting</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benchmarks.</span>
We evaluate object counting on CountQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">100</a>]</cite>, PixMo-Count <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, CLOC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>, KubriCount <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>]</cite>, and MixCount <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. CountQA and PixMo-Count focus on counting in natural images, while CLOC evaluates cross-domain generalization. KubriCount stresses multi-grained target specification with controlled distractors, and MixCount targets fine-grained open-vocabulary counting in mixed-object scenes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metrics.</span>
For models evaluated through detection output, we use the number of predicted target boxes as the count; for models that return a count directly, we extract the numerical answer from the response. We report exact-match accuracy: the percentage of queries for which the predicted count equals the ground-truth count. Thus, an error of even one object is counted as incorrect, and higher values are better.</p>
