<section id="S5.SS1.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.4 </span>Object Pointing</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benchmarks.</span>
We evaluate object pointing on two dedicated pointing benchmarks, PointBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> and PixMo-Points <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. We also evaluate pointing on the detection benchmarks COCO, LVIS, Dense200, and VisDrone, following the protocol of Rex-Omni <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metrics.</span>
A predicted point is correct when it falls inside the corresponding ground-truth mask (or the annotated target box where masks are unavailable). Each target contributes at most one true positive, so duplicate points reduce precision and missed targets reduce recall. We report F1@Point as the harmonic mean of precision and recall. For detection-derived benchmarks, precision and recall are first averaged across categories.</p>
</div>
