<section id="S5.SS1.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.5 </span>OCR</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benchmarks.</span>
We evaluate text detection and recognition on HierText <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>]</cite>, ICDAR2015 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>, TotalText <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, and SROIE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>. These benchmarks cover dense document text, incidental scene text, curved text, and structured receipts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metrics.</span>
We formulate OCR as a grounding task in which the recognized transcription serves as the category label. A prediction is considered correct only when its text exactly matches the ground-truth transcription and its box satisfies the IoU threshold. We then compute precision, recall, and F1 using the same matching procedure as object detection, and report F1 at IoU=0.5, at IoU=0.95, and averaged over thresholds from 0.5 to 0.95.</p>
</div>
