<div id="S5.SS1.SSS6.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span> As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ 5.1.6 Referring Segmentation ‣ 5.1 Main Results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ WeVisOne-VL: Unified Visual Perceptionfrom Objects to Pixels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, WeVisOne-VL improves over all baselines across every RefCOCO-family split. It also demonstrates strong potential for reasoning segmentation even without explicit chain-of-thought (CoT) reasoning. Furthermore, the results on Ref-ZOM reveal that existing segmentation models tend to output a mask even when the target is absent, lacking the ability to reject negative expressions. In contrast, WeVisOne-VL can not only output accurate masks when the target is present, but also correctly reject the expression when it is absent.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS7" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.7 </span>Promptable Matting</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Matting predicts a continuous alpha matte that preserves fine structures and partial transparency, such as hair and fur. Existing matting evaluations typically assume a salient foreground <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite> or provide a ground-truth mask to specify the target <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">116</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">91</a>]</cite>. To evaluate promptable matting in a more practical setting, we use boxes, category names, and referring expressions to specify the target object.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS7.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benchmarks and Baselines.</span>
For box prompts, we use P3M-500-NP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>, AM-2K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite>, and PPM-100 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>. For text prompts, we use our in-house benchmark WeRefMat. For the three established benchmarks, all methods use the same ground-truth box as prompt, and all baselines are run from their official checkpoints. We evaluate three SAM2-Matting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">91</a>]</cite> variants based on SAM2.1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">85</a>]</cite> and SAM3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. For box prompts, SAM2-Matting first uses SAM2.1 or SAM3 to predict a mask from the ground-truth box and then performs matting, while WeVisOne-VL constructs a sequence prefix from the ground-truth box, generates the subsequent <span id="S5.SS1.SSS7.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;OBJ&gt;</span> token, and passes its hidden state to the pixel decoder to predict the alpha matte. On WeRefMat, we compare with SAM3’s native text grounding and two MLLM-based localization settings, in which Qwen3-VL-8B-Instruct or Qwen3.5-9B first localizes the target and passes the resulting box to SAM2-Matting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS7.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS7.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metrics.</span>
We report mean absolute difference (MAD), mean squared error (MSE), gradient error (Grad), connectivity error (Conn), and sum of absolute differences (SAD) between the predicted and ground-truth alpha mattes. Following common practice, MAD and MSE are multiplied by <math id="S5.SS1.SSS7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. Lower values indicate better performance for all five metrics.</p>
</div>
