<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Visual Grounding.</span>
Open-vocabulary detectors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>, <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite> align object localization with category names and referring expressions. Grounded multimodal models then brought spatial outputs into a language interface. Kosmos-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">78</a>]</cite> represents text–box links with location tokens, while Florence-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">111</a>]</cite> casts detection, grounding, and other vision tasks as prompt-conditioned sequence generation. Molmo/PixMo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> further showed the value of explicit point supervision for grounding and counting. More recently, Rex-Omni <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> uses quantized coordinate tokens and geometry-aware reinforcement learning for detection, referring, pointing, and OCR. LocateAnything <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">104</a>]</cite> decodes whole boxes and points in parallel to improve grounding speed and precision.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Referring Segmentation.</span>
SAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> and SAM 2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">85</a>]</cite> established promptable mask prediction, and SAM 3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> extends this line to concept-prompted detection, segmentation, and tracking. To connect language reasoning with pixels, LISA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite> maps a generated segmentation token to a mask decoder, GLaMM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">84</a>]</cite> interleaves text and grounded masks, and GSVA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">110</a>]</cite> supports multiple referents and explicit rejection of absent targets. Later work explores different output representations and task mixtures: Text4Seg <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite> serializes masks as text, and UniPixel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>]</cite> integrates masks and pixel-level reasoning. Sa2VA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">122</a>]</cite> couples an MLLM with SAM 2, while Youtu-VL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">107</a>]</cite> trains visual and language tokens under unified autoregressive supervision.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Instance Matting.</span>
Image matting estimates continuous opacity for fine structures and translucent boundaries. Automatic methods such as MODNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> and P3M <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite> focus on portrait foregrounds. Matte Anything <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">117</a>]</cite> and Matting Anything <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite> use promptable segmentation to guide alpha prediction, while In-Context Matting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> specifies the foreground through a prompted reference image. SAM2-Matting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">91</a>]</cite> adds matting components to a segmentation tracker. For video, MatAnyone <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">116</a>]</cite> propagates the matte of a target specified by a first-frame mask. MatAnyone 2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">115</a>]</cite> improves detail and temporal consistency through learned quality evaluation and larger real-world training data. Many matting evaluations assume a salient foreground <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite> or provide a ground-truth mask prompt <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">116</a>]</cite>. We evaluate promptable matting in multi-object scenes using boxes, category names, and referring expressions to specify targets, with WeVisOne-VL predicting their continuous alpha mattes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Limitations</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we focus on fundamental visual perception, which entails several trade-offs. First, the limited amount of general-purpose training data may compromise the general capabilities relative to the base model. Second, for inference efficiency, we train the model to directly produce structured outputs without chain-of-thought (CoT) reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>, <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, which potentially limits its ability to tackle complex reasoning tasks. Third, our current approach is limited to single-turn prediction. It is promising to integrate an agent framework to progressively refine visual understanding through multi-turn tool interactions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond these limitations, a key challenge lies in the real-world complexity. The object prompts in benchmarks are typically clear, well-formed, and unambiguous. However, real-world user instructions are often vague and hesitant. Variations in contexts or communication styles can make a straightforward task, such as locating the target, significantly more challenging. Therefore, strong intent reasoning is essential for a reliable vision system. In future work, we will continue to improve WeVisOne-VL toward addressing these limitations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S9.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduced WeVisOne-VL, a vision-language model that unifies object-level (e.g., object detection, referring localization, counting, and OCR) and pixel-level (e.g., referring segmentation, and image matting) tasks through a structured next-token interface. Dedicated coordinate tokens encode instance predictions, while an <span id="S9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;OBJ&gt;</span> token connects each localized instance to a lightweight pixel decoder for masks and alpha mattes. A three-stage supervised curriculum develops task alignment, high-resolution dense perception, and pixel-level prediction. After that, we apply mixed-task reinforcement learning to optimize the complete grounding outputs. Across the evaluated benchmarks, WeVisOne-VL achieves strong results on both object-level and pixel-level tasks. It improves mean detection F1 and counting accuracy over comparable-scale VLMs, advances text grounding and referring localization, and surpasses the evaluated baselines for referring segmentation and image matting. Qualitative examples further show that the model can localize individual instances in crowded scenes, ground expressions involving multiple objects, and recognize when a requested target is absent. By unifying these capabilities within a single language-guided model, WeVisOne-VL provides a foundation for practical visual systems.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
