<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Pixel Decoder Training</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Warm-up.</span>
After SFT-S2, we connect the VLM to a randomly initialized pixel decoder and jointly train them for 100,000 steps with segmentation supervision. The decoder learning rate is <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math> with 1,000 warmup steps. The decoder predicts masks at <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="512\times 512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>512</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512\times 512</annotation></semantics></math> resolution. After warm-up, we retain only the trained decoder and <span id="A2.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;OBJ&gt;</span> embedding and attach them to the original SFT-S2 checkpoint for joint training. The other VLM weights updated during warm-up are discarded.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Joint training.</span>
During SFT-S3, we alternate NTP and segmentation steps at a 1:1 ratio to jointly train the SFT-S2 checkpoint with the warmed-up decoder. The VLM and decoder learning rates are <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, with a 3% warmup ratio. For each packed sequence on each GPU, we randomly sample 24 <span id="A2.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;OBJ&gt;</span> tokens and pass their corresponding representations to the decoder for segmentation prediction, while retaining the full text sequence for NTP.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Matting fine-tuning.</span>
After training is complete, we freeze the VLM and fine-tune the decoder for matting for 100,000 steps at an output resolution of <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1024\times 1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1024</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1024\times 1024</annotation></semantics></math>. We use a learning rate of <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-7}</annotation></semantics></math> for the decoder and <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.5\times 10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.5\times 10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math> for the coarse-to-fine refinement branch, with 200 warmup steps. Each GPU processes up to eight <span id="A2.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;OBJ&gt;</span> tokens. The matting loss is defined in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Training Pipeline ‣ WeVisOne-VL: Unified Visual Perceptionfrom Objects to Pixels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The warm-up and matting phases use AdamW with cosine learning-rate decay, a weight decay of 0.05, and a maximum gradient norm of 5.0. Both phases use BF16 precision and DDP, with a token budget of 98,304 per GPU, a maximum sequence length of 8,192, and 16–1,024 visual tokens per image.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>RL Rewards</h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For detection, dense detection, and referring localization, we use the grounding reward defined in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Training Pipeline ‣ WeVisOne-VL: Unified Visual Perceptionfrom Objects to Pixels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. For segmentation and matting, RL also uses only the box-based grounding reward; no reward is computed from the predicted masks or alpha mattes. For pointing, we compute F1 through one-to-one matching between predicted points and ground-truth masks, counting a match when the point lies inside the mask. Referring segmentation requires each predicted box to be followed by an <span id="A2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;OBJ&gt;</span> token, while OCR uses its transcription reward. When the target is absent, the reward is 1 only if the model predicts no object.</p>
</div>
